/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - For a linear elastic body, ∂U/∂P = Δ at the load P, along P; ∂U/∂M = θ.
   - U is strain energy (J); P is load (N); Δ is displacement (m) along P; M is moment (N·m); θ is rotation (rad).
   - Load and deflection grow together, so U = ½PΔ.
   - An aluminium strut has L = 1.2 m, A = 250 mm², E = 70 GPa.
   - Read backwards, ∂U/∂Δ = P, Castigliano's first theorem.
   - The theorem needs a load where you want the deflection.
   - Set Q = 0 inside U first, and ∂U/∂Q = 0.
   - A reaction acts at a support; ∂U/∂R gives the support's own movement, zero on a rigid support.
   - Writing U in full and then differentiating is slow.
   - N is the member force and ∂N/∂P its rate of change with the load P; M is the bending moment at x and ∂M/∂P its rate of change with P; L, A, E, I as before.
*/
